# Docker build context for the localnet stack.
#
# Every Rust service in infra/localnet/docker-compose.yml builds with
# `context: '../..'` — this directory — and its Dockerfile does `COPY . .`.
# So without this file, each build ships the whole checkout to the daemon:
# 90.9 GB across 868k files measured on the base checkout, and an operator
# watched one collector build climb past 40 GB before killing it.
#
# The Rust builds need the workspace and nothing else: Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock,
# rust-toolchain.toml, and the 13 workspace members. `cargo chef` reconstructs
# the dependency cache inside the image, so the host's target/ is not just
# unnecessary — shipping it is what makes the context enormous.
#
# `.claude/tools/docker_context.py` guards this file. Be precise about what it
# actually asserts, because the `(REQUIRED)` markers below are a READER's aid
# and nothing parses them: the guard compares against its own
# `REQUIRED_PATTERNS` dict. A test in test_docker_context.py asserts the two
# agree, so the markers cannot drift from the dict silently — but adding a
# marker here is not what enrols a pattern in the guard. Run `--measure` to
# see what currently survives, and `make docker-context` to check.
#
# The guard also REJECTS a `!` negation anywhere in this file. The matcher
# supports negation correctly; the measurement does not, because it prunes an
# excluded directory without descending and so cannot see a re-include
# beneath one.
#
# One caveat on the pattern syntax, since it is not gitignore's: Docker cleans
# each pattern with filepath.Clean, so a trailing slash is dropped and there
# is no directory-only form. A bare `target` therefore anchors at this
# directory and would miss `bots/maker-bot/target`; `**/target` is what
# catches a tree wherever it sits.

# === Build output (REQUIRED) ===

# The Rust target tree — 12 GB in the base checkout, and the single thing
# cargo-chef exists to avoid needing from the host.
**/target

# === Agent material (REQUIRED) ===

# Excluded wholesale rather than just `.claude/worktrees`, for two reasons.
#
# The worktrees are the size argument: one full checkout per agent worktree,
# each with its own target/ and node_modules, 57 GB — the largest tree by far
# and the one a fresh clone is least likely to notice. It is kept out of git
# by .git/info/exclude, which is **user-local and uncommitted**, so nothing in
# a checkout keeps it out of a build context and no committed ignore file
# mentioned it before this one. A build must never consume another branch's
# checkout in any case.
#
# The rest of the tree is the second reason: `.claude/settings.local.json` is
# git-ignored, and Docker does NOT honor gitignore — so a per-machine config
# file would otherwise ride into every image layer. Nothing anywhere under
# `.claude/` is a build input, so the narrower pattern bought nothing.
.claude

# === Git metadata (REQUIRED) ===

# 69 MB of object store, and no build script reads it — the one build.rs in
# the workspace (programs/dropset) only expands sBPF assembly. In a worktree
# `.git` is a small file rather than a directory; the pattern covers both.
**/.git

# === Node / pnpm (REQUIRED) ===

# 1.3 GB, hoisted to the repo root by pnpm-workspace.yaml, plus the per-app
# trees. No root-context build runs pnpm: the frontend and the decks run on
# the host, and the explorer image builds from its own local context.
**/node_modules

# Next.js build output, for the frontend and the decks alike.
**/.next

# Generated in full by the frontend's predev/prebuild hooks — brand assets
# copied in, flags mirrored out of node_modules, token and wallet icons
# fetched. Nothing under it is committed (frontend/.gitignore says so).
frontend/public

# === Node / pnpm (the rest) ===

frontend/out
decks/out
**/dist
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.turbo
**/.pnpm-store

# === Secrets and machine-local state (REQUIRED) ===

# Never let a credential reach a build context: a layer is write-once, so a
# file that reaches one stays in the image even if a later stage deletes it.
# The localnet enclave file holds `op://` references rather than values, but
# it is machine-local either way, and in a worktree it is a symlink into the
# base checkout that would not resolve in the image.
#
# These are REQUIRED for a different reason than every other required
# pattern: not size, but because the context ceiling structurally cannot
# catch them. A deleted credential pattern costs a few hundred bytes against
# a 512 MB bound, so the presence check is the ONLY thing standing behind it.
**/.env
**/.env.*
infra/localnet/secrets.local.env

# The one tree in this repo whose stated purpose is holding private keys.
# Today it carries only committed localnet keypairs — no mainnet authority,
# and `**/.git` above already excludes the object store holding those same
# bytes, so this closes an inconsistency as much as a leak. It is here so the
# day someone drops a funded keypair beside AAAA.json, the build context is
# not the thing that has to be remembered.
keys

# === Machine-local runtime state ===

# Not REQUIRED: these hold neither credentials nor enough bytes to matter, so
# neither the presence check nor the ceiling has a reason to enrol them. They
# are excluded because they are machine-local and simply not build inputs.
# (See .gitignore for what each one holds.)
.audit-loop
.maker-bot

# === Presentation and asset trees ===

# Neither is a Rust build input. The decks tree is 46 MB and the brand assets
# are consumed by the frontend's copy script on the host, not in an image.
decks
brand-assets

# === Editor and OS litter ===

**/.DS_Store
**/.idea
**/.vscode
**/*.swp
